RFC-003
JGPU Memory & Resource Model

Status: Draft

Author: Mike McCollum

Requires: RFC-001, RFC-002

Version: 0.1

Abstract

This RFC defines the JGPU memory system.

Memory is treated as an explicit resource owned by a Device and managed through Allocators. Resources expose typed views over memory while preserving backend independence.

The objective is to provide deterministic behavior across software and hardware implementations while remaining compatible with modern GPU architectures.

Motivation

Modern compute APIs expose memory models that differ substantially between vendors.

Some implementations hide residency.

Others require explicit placement.

Some permit unified memory.

Others expose separate device memory.

JGPU must support all of these without changing application behavior.

The memory model therefore describes ownership and guarantees rather than physical implementation.

Design Goals

The memory system shall be:

Explicit
Deterministic
Portable
Thread-safe
Backend independent
Zero-copy where possible
Serializable
Observable

Memory ownership must always be knowable by the runtime.

Implicit ownership transfer is prohibited.

Core Concepts

The memory hierarchy consists of six primary objects.

Device

↓

Allocator

↓

Allocation

↓

Resource

↓

View

↓

Binding

Each layer has a single responsibility.

Device Ownership

Every allocation belongs to exactly one Device.

Device A

└── Memory

Resources cannot exist without a Device.

Destroying the Device invalidates every resource it owns.

Allocators

Allocators manage physical memory.

Responsibilities include

Allocation
Reuse
Pooling
Defragmentation
Residency management
Statistics
Lifetime tracking

Allocators do not expose hardware-specific APIs.

Allocation

An Allocation represents raw memory.

Properties include

Size
Alignment
Visibility
Residency
Usage flags

Allocations have no interpretation.

They are simply bytes.

Resources

Resources provide typed access to allocations.

Examples include

Buffer
Image
Texture
Structured Buffer
Constant Buffer
Acceleration Structure
Indirect Command Buffer

Resources define interpretation.

Allocations define storage.

Resource Views

Views expose subsets or alternate interpretations of resources.

Examples include

Entire Buffer

↓

Slice

↓

Read-only View

↓

Writable View

Multiple views may reference the same underlying resource.

Views never own memory.

Binding

Binding associates resources with execution.

Kernel

↓

Binding Table

↓

Resources

Bindings are immutable during execution.

Changing bindings requires a new dispatch.

Memory Types

JGPU defines logical memory classes.

Device Local

Optimized for execution.

CPU visibility is optional.

Host Visible

CPU accessible.

Suitable for staging and uploads.

Shared

Visible to both CPU and Device.

Performance is implementation dependent.

Temporary

Short-lived transient allocations.

Automatically reclaimed.

Persistent

Long-lived application resources.

Explicit destruction required.

Residency

Residency determines whether memory is available for execution.

Residency may be

Resident
Evicted
Pending
Unknown

Applications may request residency.

Backends determine implementation.

Mapping

Applications may request mapped access.

Map()

↓

CPU Pointer

↓

Modify

↓

Flush

↓

Unmap()

Mapping semantics are backend independent.

The runtime performs any required synchronization.

Coherency

Mapped memory may be

Coherent
Non-coherent

Applications must explicitly flush non-coherent ranges.

This behavior remains consistent across every backend.

Buffer Model

Buffers represent linear memory.

Properties include

Size
Alignment
Usage
Visibility
Access flags

Buffers contain no intrinsic format.

Images

Images represent multidimensional storage.

Properties include

Width
Height
Depth
Layers
Mip levels
Format

Images are immutable after creation except for contents.

Resource States

Every resource exists in a defined state.

Created

↓

Allocated

↓

Bound

↓

Ready

↓

Executing

↓

Available

↓

Destroyed

Transitions occur only through runtime operations.

Resource Hazards

The runtime tracks

Read-after-write
Write-after-read
Write-after-write

Hazard resolution is deterministic.

Backends may optimize internally but must preserve behavior.

Aliasing

Multiple resources may reference one allocation.

Aliasing is explicit.

Undefined aliasing behavior is prohibited.

Lifetime

Every resource follows deterministic ownership.

Create

↓

Use

↓

Release

↓

Destroy

Reference counting is permitted.

Garbage collection is optional.

Applications must never depend upon deferred destruction.

Resource Handles

Applications interact with opaque handles.

Handles remain valid until destruction.

Pointer stability is never guaranteed.

Alignment

Every backend reports required alignment.

Applications may query

minimum_buffer_alignment

constant_alignment

texture_alignment

page_size

Applications must not assume fixed values.

Transfers

Transfers occur explicitly.

Supported operations include

Copy
Upload
Download
Resolve
Fill

Implicit transfers are prohibited unless documented by capability.

Memory Budget

Devices expose runtime statistics.

Example

Total Memory

Available Memory

Resident Memory

Allocated Memory

Evicted Memory

Statistics are advisory.

Applications must not rely on exact values.

Debug Validation

Debug mode validates

Double mapping
Invalid bindings
Out-of-bounds views
Lifetime violations
Alignment errors
Resource leaks
Hazard violations

Validation must not alter execution behavior.

Serialization

Every resource description is serializable.

Serialized descriptions exclude physical memory contents unless explicitly requested.

This enables

Replay
Offline compilation
Remote execution
Deterministic testing
Backend Responsibilities

Backends are responsible for

Physical allocation
Residency implementation
Platform synchronization
Native resource creation
Performance optimization

Backends may not alter public memory semantics.

Non-Goals

This RFC does not define

Heap allocation strategies
Garbage collection algorithms
Compression
Virtual memory implementation
NUMA policies
Backend optimization techniques

These remain implementation details.

Rationale

The JGPU memory model separates storage, interpretation, and execution into distinct concepts.

An Allocation owns bytes.

A Resource defines what those bytes represent.

A View defines how they are accessed.

A Binding determines how they participate in execution.

This separation allows JGPU to target hardware with vastly different memory architectures while presenting applications with a stable and deterministic programming model.

One architectural suggestion

I'd introduce one concept now that will pay dividends across the entire stack:

Memory Domains

Instead of thinking only in terms of buffers and textures, define domains:

Host Domain
Shared Domain
Device Domain
Transient Domain
Remote Domain   (future)
Persistent Domain

Then every resource belongs to a domain rather than directly to a hardware memory type. That gives you room for future RFCs on distributed execution and your "virtual GPU" without changing the API. A remote node simply exposes another memory domain, and the same allocation/binding semantics still apply.

That's the kind of abstraction that tends to age well because it's describing ownership and visibility, not today's hardware. It keeps the contract stable even as the implementations evolve.
